Croce's inspiration for the song was a friend he met in his brief time in the [[United States Army|US Army]]:

{{Quote|I met him at [[Fort Dix]], [[New Jersey]]. We were in [[lineman (technician)|lineman]] (telephone) school together. He stayed there about a week, and one evening he turned around and said he was really fed up and tired. He went [[AWOL]], and then came back at the end of the month to get his paycheck. They put handcuffs on him and took him away. Just to listen to him talk and see how 'bad' he was, I knew someday I was gonna write a song about him.<ref name="superseventies">{{cite web |year=2007 |url = http://www.superseventies.com/1973_2singles.html|title = Bad, Bad Leroy Brown |website = superseventies.com| access-date = 2008-03-14 | last= Croce| first=Jim }}</ref>}}

Croce told a variation of this story on ''[[The Helen Reddy Show]]'' in July 1973:

{{Quote|This is a song about a guy I was in the army with&nbsp;...  It was at Fort Dix, in New Jersey, that I met this guy.  He was not made to climb the tree of knowledge, as they say, but he was strong, so nobody'd ever told him what to do, and after about a week down there he said "Later for this" and decided to go home.  So he went AWOL—which means to take your own vacation—and he did. But he made the mistake of coming back at the end of the month to get his paycheck.  I don't know if you've ever seen handcuffs put on anybody, but it was SNAP and that was the end of it for a good friend of mine, who I wrote this tune about, named Leroy Brown.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DOqrYmV1Xiw |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/varchive/youtube/20211219/DOqrYmV1Xiw |archive-date=2021-12-19 |url-status=live|work=The Helen Reddy Show|date= July 1973|publisher= video clip on YouTube|title= Jim Croce on "The Helen Reddy Show" U.S. TV 1974 (2 songs) |access-date= August 26, 2016}}{{cbignore}}</ref>}}

Croce explained the chorus reference to Leroy Brown being "meaner than a [[Wreck yard|junkyard]] dog":

{{Quote|Yeah, I spent about a year and a half driving those $29 cars, so I drove around a lot looking for a [[universal joint]] for a '57 Chevy [[panel truck]] or a transmission for a '51 [[Dodge]]. I got to know many junkyards well, and they all have those dogs in them. They all have either an axle tied around their necks or an old lawnmower to keep 'em at least slowed down a bit, so you have a decent chance of getting away from them.<ref name="superseventies"/>}}

